 economy grew in the 2000s people  had to deal with the effects of  digitisation ecommerce and the  consolidation of retailers into a  handful of competitors and here I think  the office speaks to the both possession  of the economy that's an academic term  by the way that is at least the argument  proposed by anthropologist David Graeber  who claims that BS jobs are  proliferating throughout the developed  world he defines a BS job as a form of  paid employment that is so completely  pointless unnecessary or pernicious that  even the employee cannot justify its  existence even though as part of the  conditions of employment the employee  feels obliged to pretend that this is  not the case  and this seems to be the crux of life at  dunder-mifflin sitting in useless  meetings bow body  bow body what is the first B stand for  what are we doing we're making acronyms  in a job that's nearing obsolescence  following the random BS directives of  your superiors we're throwing out the  entire playbook we're starting from  scratch we're implementing a brand new  system and trying desperately to find  meaning in it all do you find that your  life feels pointless now that nobody's  actually filming you anymore yes there  might be some debate whether the workers  of dunder-mifflin have jobs or both jobs  which is an important distinction a  table may just sucks  say getting paid minimum wage to scrub  the toilet bowls at Taco Bell but you  know someone's got to do it and I don't  mean to say that there isn't real work  being done at dunder-mifflin Oscar and  Angela seem to be actually competent  accountants who get people paid on time  the sales people sell and so on but the  office checks off many of Graeter's  boxes for a BS job many of its employees  are aware of the general pointlessness  of their work Dunder Mifflin can't  compete with the modern chance and  management is unwilling or unable to  adapt at any moment Dunder Mifflin could  close and their clients would simply